  "response": "I’ll run the scan and check the flagged code, then group the proposals by finding type and explain the concrete risks for an agent extending these modules.\n\nThe scan matches your report: 82 findings overall, with eight in the three categories you highlighted. Several need qualification: `sveltePlugin` already returns `Plugin`, and the scanner counts nested callback returns against it; `buildStart` is a Rollup hook name. The clearer risks are mutation of shared options, broad worker payloads, and cleanup behavior hidden inside the orchestration.\n\nI ran `crimes scan --format json`. It reproduces **82 findings**, including **23 in these files** and **eight matching the three categories you highlighted**. Source inspection shows some scanner overreach, so the refactor should clarify real contracts rather than mechanically eliminate warnings.\n\n| Finding type | ID | Location | Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00003` | `src/rollup.ts:38–159` — `getRollupConfig` | Low |\n| | `crime_00008` | `src/rollup.ts:77–81` — `buildStart` | Low |\n| | `crime_00014` | `src/index.ts:167–459` — `build` | Medium |\n| | `crime_00015` | `src/index.ts:291–365` — `buildAll` | Low |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `crime_00007` | `src/rollup.ts:38–159` — `getRollupConfig` | Low |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | `crime_00011` | `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9–124` — `sveltePlugin` | Low |\n| | `crime_00012` | `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:17–122` — `setup` | Low |\n| | `crime_00013` | `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:31–121` — load callback | Low |\n\n1. **`name_behavior_mismatch`: expose execution and cleanup boundaries.**\n\n   In [src/rollup.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-1gZjIX/src/rollup.ts:38), rename `getRollupConfig` to `loadDtsRollupConfig`: it reads project metadata, changes global logging state, and mutates `options.dts.entry`. Move logging configuration to the execution boundary and construct a fresh declaration configuration. Extract configuration assembly from metadata loading.\n\n   Keep the framework-required `buildStart` hook name, delegating to `cleanDeclarationOutputs`. **Deletion happens when that hook runs**, not when the configuration is constructed.\n\n   In [src/index.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-1gZjIX/src/index.ts:167), preserve the public `build` API and explicitly declare `Promise<void>`. Rename internal `buildAll` to `runBuildCycle`, with named steps for previous-process cleanup, output cleaning, format builds, public-file copying, and success hooks.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** an agent could reuse configuration loading as a harmless inspection operation, move deletion outside its lifecycle hook, or parallelize cleanup with output writes. The scanner’s assumption that every `build*` name implies purity is too broad; exposing sequencing is more valuable than renaming the public API.\n\n2. **`option_bag_junk_drawer`: narrow ownership and distinguish raw, resolved, and worker inputs.**\n\n   `getRollupConfig` already accepts `NormalizedOptions`; its shape is **broad, not implicit**. Replace that dependency with named records for declaration inputs, declaration outputs, and cleanup policy. Keep watch mode and logging configuration in the runner. Use readonly nested records and copy arrays before transformation.\n\n   Define an explicit, cloneable `DtsWorkerRequest` shared by `index.ts` and `rollup.ts`. Currently, the parent spreads all normalized options and manually removes function-valued fields. Selecting supported fields explicitly prevents a future callback option from accidentally entering `postMessage`.\n\n   Give `normalizeOptions` an explicit `Promise<NormalizedOptions>` result and construct validated required fields instead of asserting a `Partial<NormalizedOptions>` into completeness. Separate raw JSON compiler options from parsed `ts.CompilerOptions`.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** an agent could assume every public option works inside the declaration worker, introduce a structured-clone failure, or mutate caller-owned configuration. Two existing examples deserve attention: Rollup rewrites `dts.entry`, and `buildAll` uses `unshift` on an array that can alias `options.clean`.\n\n3. **`return_shape_roulette`: type each callback according to its own protocol.**\n\n   In [src/esbuild/svelte.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-1gZjIX/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9), `sveltePlugin` **already declares `: Plugin`**, and `setup` registers callbacks without returning their results. The scanner aggregates nested returns, including diagnostics and preprocessing results.\n\n   Extract explicitly typed helpers:\n\n   - `resolveSvelteCss`: `OnResolveArgs → OnResolveResult`.\n   - `loadSvelteComponent`: `OnLoadArgs → Promise<OnLoadResult>`.\n   - `toEsbuildDiagnostic`: guarded compiler diagnostic/error input → `PartialMessage`, with source and filename passed explicitly.\n   - A script preprocessor typed using Svelte’s preprocessing contract.\n\n   Preserve esbuild’s success and error result shapes. Introduce a discriminated union only if an internal compilation helper needs one, then translate it at the esbuild boundary.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** an agent could confuse preprocessing `{ code, map }` with load `{ contents, warnings }`, assume failed loads contain `contents`, or lose diagnostic locations. Combining all nested returns into one universal result type would obscure these distinct contracts.\n\nThe other **15 findings** support targeted follow-up: ten `large_function` alerts and one `large_file` alert favor these responsibility-based extractions; two connectivity alerts call for dependency review. Rollup’s clock reads concern duration reporting. Its swallowed-error alert also needs qualification: `handleError(error)` logs the error and sends a worker error message, but `runRollup` then resolves, allowing a subsequent success message. Give the worker one explicit terminal outcome.\n\nImplement contracts first, then extract and rename. Validate cleanup timing and declaration preservation, repeated rebuilds without option mutation, worker failure signaling, and Svelte success/error/CSS behavior. Run TypeScript checking, the relevant build/declaration/Svelte tests, and the scan again. **No source changes were made.**",
